return array (
  'content' => '<p>Pull handle length on a main entrance door affects grip comfort, visual balance and perceived luxury. Too short on a 2.7 m tall leaf looks like an afterthought; too long on a modest apartment door overwhelms the panel. Use door height, leaf width and symmetry rules to choose length before holes are cut in PVD stainless.</p><h2>Proportion to door height</h2><p>A common starting point: pull length roughly 25–35% of door height for vertical centre-mounted pulls on single leaves. A 2400 mm door often suits 600–850 mm pulls depending on design boldness.</p><p>Double-leaf entries may use two vertical pulls of equal length or one long pull on active leaf only — symmetry vs function.</p><h2>Single vs offset double pulls</h2><p>One centred vertical pull suits minimalist doors. Offset pairs (upper and lower) suit very tall hotel doors for ergonomic reach — less common in typical residential entries.</p><p>Horizontal pulls across door width appear on contemporary commercial entries; ensure structural backing for torque on wide leaves.</p><h2>Handedness and daily use</h2><p>Active leaf pull should fall naturally at push/pull side — confirm swing direction on shop drawings. Family members of varied height benefit from pulls mounted slightly below geometric centre.</p><p>Coordinate with <a href="/studio/main-entrance-pvd-doors">main entrance PVD doors</a> fabrication schedule.</p><h2>Finish and section profile</h2><p>Round section pulls feel classic; square or rectangular profiles read architectural. Larger diameter improves grip for gloved security staff on commercial projects.</p><p>Match PVD finish to door frame via <a href="/shop/door-handles">door handles</a> from same batch where possible.</p><h2>Installation constraints</h2><p>Through-bolts need door core or reinforcement plate. Glass-infill doors require pull mounting into solid frame zones only — never glass alone.</p><p>Lock height and vision panel location must clear pull backplate — share elevation with fabricator early.</p><h2>Reference and ordering</h2><p>See adjacent projects on <a href="/projects">projects page</a> for proportion cues.</p><p>Vyomika Atelier advises pull length with door orders from our Delhi, India studio — send door elevation PDF with enquiry.</p><h2>Measuring door height correctly</h2><p>Measure finished floor to underside of head — not rough opening. Raised thresholds and stone saddles affect perceived door height and pull placement.</p><p>Double-height lobby doors may need longer pulls than bedroom-scale leaves even when leaf width is similar — proportion is height-driven first.</p><h2>Mock-up on site before drilling</h2><p>Use painter\'s tape to outline pull length and centreline on door face. Photograph from entry approach and from interior side before approving hole positions.</p><p>Security cameras and doorbells mounted beside pulls should not force off-centre placement that looks accidental — integrate devices in elevation drawing.</p><h2>Paired pulls on double-leaf entries</h2><p>Active and passive leaves often carry matching vertical pulls for symmetry. Passive leaf may be fixed — pull still provides visual balance even if unused daily.</p><p>Narrow sidelight combinations reduce solid door width — shorter pull may read better than formula suggests; trust mock-up over rule of thumb alone.</p><h2>Accessibility considerations</h2><p>Pulls should be graspable without excessive reach — elderly users benefit from pulls mounted slightly below geometric centre on tall doors.</p><p>Contrast between PVD finish and door face helps visually impaired users locate hardware — matte black on light stone-infill doors is a common pairing.</p><h2>Visual weight versus door panel design</h2><p>Full-height glass doors with narrow steel stiles suit shorter pulls or offset vertical pulls that align with stile width. Solid metal doors with inset panels can carry longer pulls without overpowering the composition. Etched or layered patterns on <a href="/blog/stainless-steel-glass-etched-entrance-doors-compared">entrance door faces</a> may suggest centered pulls that respect symmetry axes in the design.</p><h2>Commercial and hospitality entries</h2><p>Hotel and restaurant main doors see gloved staff and luggage contact — larger diameter pulls improve grip and reduce perceived flimsiness. Coordinate pull length with automatic operator locations and vestibule depth so door swing and pull placement feel natural to arriving guests.</p><h3>Retrofit on existing doors</h3><p>Replacing handles on installed doors requires checking core material and previous bore spacing. Cover plates can hide old holes only within limits — confirm with fabricator before promising invisible retrofit.</p><h2>Coordination with door thickness and inset panels</h2><p>Thick solid-metal doors accept through-bolted long pulls with stable backing. Thin flush doors or glass-dominant leaves need pulls sized to mount within reinforced stile zones — length may be limited by structure rather than aesthetics alone.</p><p>Share door section detail with handle supplier before finalizing pull length. For full entrance specification context, read <a href="/blog/how-to-choose-luxury-main-entrance-door">how to choose a luxury main entrance door</a>.</p><h2>Weather and exposure at entrance</h2><p>External-facing pulls on semi-covered entries still see dust and occasional rain splash. PVD finishes tolerate typical Indian entrance exposure when substrates are stainless — avoid ferrous pull bodies on uncoated mild steel in coastal zones without project-specific review.</p><p>Pair pull selection with threshold and canopy design so users grip dry hardware when possible — comfort and perceived quality improve together.</p><h2>Double-height lobby proportion</h2><p>Tall lobby doors above standard floor-to-floor height often need pulls toward the upper end of the 25–35% height range — or paired vertical pulls on exceptionally wide leaves. Mock up with tape from the viewing distance of the main approach path, not only from close range during installation.</p><p>Record approved pull length on the door schedule with a dimensioned sketch — installers should not guess from catalogue thumbnails alone on custom-height leaves.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Choose main door pull length from door height, leaf count and visual weight of the façade. Centre-mounted vertical pulls near 25–35% of door height are a reliable starting range — then adjust with mock-up tape on site before drilling.</p><p>Browse <a href="/shop/door-handles">door handles</a>.</p><p>Read <a href="/blog/how-to-choose-luxury-main-entrance-door">luxury entrance door guide</a>.</p><p>Compare door faces in <a href="/blog/stainless-steel-glass-etched-entrance-doors-compared">steel, glass and etched doors</a>.</p><p>For a project-specific quotation, share floor plans or photos through our <a href="/contact">contact page</a>. Architects and interior designers may also begin from the <a href="/professionals">professionals collaboration page</a>.</p>',
